## Agent behaviour

<!-- Never externalise this section. It applies on every turn, whatever file is
     being touched. Rules below are ordered by how often they bite. -->

### 1. Think before coding

Do not assume. State assumptions before acting. On an ambiguous request, present the competing readings and ask rather than guess. Do not read "do X" as licence to do X plus Y plus Z.

### 2. Simplicity first

The minimum code that solves the stated problem. No unrequested features, no abstraction for single-use code, no anticipated flexibility, no error handling for impossible states.

### 3. Surgical changes

Touch only what the request requires. Do not improve adjacent working code. Follow the existing style. Remove only the orphans your own change created. Test: every modified line traces to the request.

### 4. Goal-directed execution

Turn tasks into checkable success criteria ("add validation" becomes "write the invalid-input tests, then make them pass"). Brief plan for multi-step work, one verification per step.

### 5. Branch discipline

Before the first modification of a task: `git branch --show-current`, then `git status --short`. Never infer the branch from the conversation or from an earlier observation.

**Never modify `{{PROD_BRANCH}}` directly** without an explicit request for that specific change. If you are on it: stop, say so, propose a working branch, wait.

**Never switch branches without approval.** `checkout`, `switch`, `reset`, `pull --rebase`, `stash` change what every session sharing this directory sees.

{{#OUTPUT_DENSITY}}
### 6. Output mode

Engineering-report prose: result first, no pleasantries, no restatement of the request, no closing summary. Every proposed code change carries a **one-sentence** technical rationale naming the criterion (SOLID, DRY, performance, type safety, layer boundary), except mechanical edits. Critical peer review: state flaws plainly, including in existing code and in the request itself.

Bounds: density applies to **prose**, never to the **work**. It never licenses skipping clarifying questions, hiding a risk, or calling something done that is not.
{{/OUTPUT_DENSITY}}

